<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1504</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imad-shahi-dynasty">Imad Shahi dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>. Born a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> in the lands of Vijayanagara, he was captured as a boy, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and rose in Bahmani service to become governor of Berar. Around 1490, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> broke apart, he declared independence, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalpur">Ellichpur</a> and creating the smallest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fathullah-imad-ul-mulk.jpg" alt="Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hrishikesh Mahajan · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavilgad3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
